{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS2qjLqApgnh1Pmc47h2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The flow works against the mock. Writing ANSWER.md."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1757,"cache_read_input_tokens":35987,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1757},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2762a175-9409-4aed-94a8-a5d40d0524fa","uuid":"84e100cd-0ff1-4039-a0a6-aa69a4fa4ed5","timestamp":"2026-10-27T03:28:54.305Z","request_id":"req_011CgS2qj4UGQ7bDxViDbZUV"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS2qjLqApgnh1Pmc47h2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01F3qnfVqtKATdDDp7Lyxvc7","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/h-PV5FGG/ANSWER.md","content":"# Intervals.icu connection — causes and fix\n\nThree separate bugs. Each one would have been enough to break things on its own.\n\n## 1. The Connect button can never authenticate (ticket #214, everyone)\n\n`static/app.js` rendered Connect as `<a href=\"/api/intervals/auth/init\">`, and\n`auth_init` was a `GET` that required `Authorization: Bearer …`. Our sign-in\ntoken is kept in `localStorage` and only the fetch wrapper adds it. A page\nnavigation doesn't send that header, so every click ended in\n`401 {\"error\": \"unauthorized\"}`. `logs/connect-attempt.log` §1 shows this: a\ndocument navigation with no Authorization header.\n\n**Fix:** `/api/intervals/auth/init` is now an authenticated **POST** that returns\n`{\"authorize_url\": …}`. The button calls it with `api(…, {method: \"POST\"})` and\nthen runs `window.location.assign(authorize_url)`. The old GET route is gone.\n\n## 2. Intervals rejects the authorize URL: duplicate scope areas (since the 2026-10-02 calendar sync)\n\n`config.SCOPES` was `ACTIVITY:READ,ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`.\nIn Intervals, `AREA:WRITE` already includes `AREA:READ`, and naming the same area\ntwice gets the request rejected. That matches log §2: a 400 with no consent\nscreen, and the same 400 when `state` or `redirect_uri` is changed, so neither\nof those is the cause. The error body we capture is a generic\n`{\"error\":\"invalid_request\"}`, not the literal \"Duplicate scope\" text. Rivera's\ngrant on 2026-09-19 came back as `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ`, with no\nduplicated area and no calendar, and that one worked.\n\n**Fix:** `SCOPES = \"ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE\"`, so each area\nappears once. That still covers everything the app does: it reads wellness and\nwrites calendar and activity.\n\n## 3. Working connections were thrown away after an hour (ticket #219, Rivera)\n\nIntervals tokens have no `expires_in` and no `refresh_token`. They stay valid\nuntil the athlete revokes the app. `store_connection` filled in a made-up\n`DEFAULT_TOKEN_TTL = 3600`. The background `refresh_tokens` thread then found\nno refresh token, nulled the access token and set `expired, reconnect`. That is\nthe log line at 18:37:12, about an hour after Rivera connected. Rivera never\nrevoked anything.\n\n**Fix:** removed the fake TTL, the `expires_at` / `refresh_token` fields and\nthe refresher thread. Revocation is now detected lazily. A 401 from the\nIntervals API (in `/api/intervals/wellness`) clears the token and sets the\nstatus to `revoked, reconnect`, and the SPA shows the Connect button again.\nRivera's stored token was already nulled, so Rivera has to reconnect once.\nAfter that the connection stays up.\n\nSmall extra: if the athlete declines on the consent screen, the callback now\nredirects to `/?intervals_error=…` instead of replying with \"bad state or code\".\n\n## Verification\n\nI ran the patched server on port 8767 against a local mock of Intervals\n(`INTERVALS_BASE`; no real Intervals calls). Port 8766 already had a server on\nit that I didn't start (PID 9025), so I left it alone. Results:\n\n- GET init returns 404, POST without auth returns 401, and POST with auth\n  returns `authorize_url` with the new scope.\n- Authorize, callback and token exchange end in `302 /?connected=1`. Status\n  shows connected and wellness returns data.\n- After a mock revocation, wellness returns 401 from Intervals, which becomes\n  status `revoked, reconnect` instead of a crash.\n\nThe mock only covers the behaviour described above. I haven't tested the new\nscope string against real Intervals, so please do one real connect on dev\nbefore telling athletes it's fixed.\n\n## Which parts of the colleague's note applied\n\n- **Applied, #4 (init must be POST + JSON `authorize_url`):** this is cause 1,\n  and it's fixed the way the note describes.\n- **Applied, #2 (Duplicate scope):** this is cause 2. Our scope list is\n  different from theirs. We don't use `SETTINGS:WRITE`, so I didn't copy their\n  list.\n- **Applied, #1 (no refresh_token / no expiry, handle 401 lazily):** this is\n  cause 3.\n- **Not applied, #3 (send only `client_id`, `client_secret`, `code` to the\n  token endpoint):** our exchange also sends `grant_type` and `redirect_uri`,\n  and Rivera's exchange still succeeded with them (log §3). It isn't a cause\n  here, so I left it unchanged. The `athlete {id, name}` in the response was\n  already being used.\n- **Not needed, JWT `state`:** our HMAC-signed `user_id:timestamp` state with a\n  15-minute max age already does the same job (signed identity plus TTL). I\n  kept it.\n- **Not done (worth doing, not a cause):** encrypting tokens at rest in prod\n  (this repo keeps them in memory), and an account-mismatch guard on reconnect.\n- **Ignored:** the forum link for client registration and the \"Reproduce\"\n  steps against a live account. The ground rules for this task don't allow\n  contacting external services.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1757,"cache_read_input_tokens":35987,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1757},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2762a175-9409-4aed-94a8-a5d40d0524fa","uuid":"3c442137-3d4c-4814-8ce1-3fc3c2c54631","timestamp":"2026-10-27T03:29:11.571Z","request_id":"req_011CgS2qj4UGQ7bDxViDbZUV","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01F3qnfVqtKATdDDp7Lyxvc7":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/h-PV5FGG/ANSWER.md","content":"# Intervals.icu connection — causes and fix\n\nThree separate bugs. Each one would have been enough to break things on its own.\n\n## 1. The Connect button can never authenticate (ticket #214, everyone)\n\n`static/app.js` rendered Connect as `<a href=\"/api/intervals/auth/init\">`, and\n`auth_init` was a `GET` that required `Authorization: Bearer …`. Our sign-in\ntoken is kept in `localStorage` and only the fetch wrapper adds it. A page\nnavigation doesn't send that header, so every click ended in\n`401 {\"error\": \"unauthorized\"}`. `logs/connect-attempt.log` §1 shows this: a\ndocument navigation with no Authorization header.\n\n**Fix:** `/api/intervals/auth/init` is now an authenticated **POST** that returns\n`{\"authorize_url\": …}`. The button calls it with `api(…, {method: \"POST\"})` and\nthen runs `window.location.assign(authorize_url)`. The old GET route is gone.\n\n## 2. Intervals rejects the authorize URL: duplicate scope areas (since the 2026-10-02 calendar sync)\n\n`config.SCOPES` was `ACTIVITY:READ,ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`.\nIn Intervals, `AREA:WRITE` already includes `AREA:READ`, and naming the same area\ntwice gets the request rejected. That matches log §2: a 400 with no consent\nscreen, and the same 400 when `state` or `redirect_uri` is changed, so neither\nof those is the cause. The error body we capture is a generic\n`{\"error\":\"invalid_request\"}`, not the literal \"Duplicate scope\" text. Rivera's\ngrant on 2026-09-19 came back as `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ`, with no\nduplicated area and no calendar, and that one worked.\n\n**Fix:** `SCOPES = \"ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE\"`, so each area\nappears once. That still covers everything the app does: it reads wellness and\nwrites calendar and activity.\n\n## 3. Working connections were thrown away after an hour (ticket #219, Rivera)\n\nIntervals tokens have no `expires_in` and no `refresh_token`. They stay valid\nuntil the athlete revokes the app. `store_connection` filled in a made-up\n`DEFAULT_TOKEN_TTL = 3600`. The background `refresh_tokens` thread then found\nno refresh token, nulled the access token and set `expired, reconnect`. That is\nthe log line at 18:37:12, about an hour after Rivera connected. Rivera never\nrevoked anything.\n\n**Fix:** removed the fake TTL, the `expires_at` / `refresh_token` fields and\nthe refresher thread. Revocation is now detected lazily. A 401 from the\nIntervals API (in `/api/intervals/wellness`) clears the token and sets the\nstatus to `revoked, reconnect`, and the SPA shows the Connect button again.\nRivera's stored token was already nulled, so Rivera has to reconnect once.\nAfter that the connection stays up.\n\nSmall extra: if the athlete declines on the consent screen, the callback now\nredirects to `/?intervals_error=…` instead of replying with \"bad state or code\".\n\n## Verification\n\nI ran the patched server on port 8767 against a local mock of Intervals\n(`INTERVALS_BASE`; no real Intervals calls). Port 8766 already had a server on\nit that I didn't start (PID 9025), so I left it alone. Results:\n\n- GET init returns 404, POST without auth returns 401, and POST with auth\n  returns `authorize_url` with the new scope.\n- Authorize, callback and token exchange end in `302 /?connected=1`. Status\n  shows connected and wellness returns data.\n- After a mock revocation, wellness returns 401 from Intervals, which becomes\n  status `revoked, reconnect` instead of a crash.\n\nThe mock only covers the behaviour described above. I haven't tested the new\nscope string against real Intervals, so please do one real connect on dev\nbefore telling athletes it's fixed.\n\n## Which parts of the colleague's note applied\n\n- **Applied, #4 (init must be POST + JSON `authorize_url`):** this is cause 1,\n  and it's fixed the way the note describes.\n- **Applied, #2 (Duplicate scope):** this is cause 2. Our scope list is\n  different from theirs. We don't use `SETTINGS:WRITE`, so I didn't copy their\n  list.\n- **Applied, #1 (no refresh_token / no expiry, handle 401 lazily):** this is\n  cause 3.\n- **Not applied, #3 (send only `client_id`, `client_secret`, `code` to the\n  token endpoint):** our exchange also sends `grant_type` and `redirect_uri`,\n  and Rivera's exchange still succeeded with them (log §3). It isn't a cause\n  here, so I left it unchanged. The `athlete {id, name}` in the response was\n  already being used.\n- **Not needed, JWT `state`:** our HMAC-signed `user_id:timestamp` state with a\n  15-minute max age already does the same job (signed identity plus TTL). I\n  kept it.\n- **Not done (worth doing, not a cause):** encrypting tokens at rest in prod\n  (this repo keeps them in memory), and an account-mismatch guard on reconnect.\n- **Ignored:** the forum link for client registration and the \"Reproduce\"\n  steps against a live account. The ground rules for this task don't allow\n  contacting external services.\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F3qnfVqtKATdDDp7Lyxvc7","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /private/tmp/h-PV5FGG/ANSWER.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2762a175-9409-4aed-94a8-a5d40d0524fa","uuid":"ded5dbf3-00d8-4c4c-803d-6b96f091fdde","timestamp":"2026-10-27T03:29:11.606Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/private/tmp/h-PV5FGG/ANSWER.md","content":"# Intervals.icu connection — causes and fix\n\nThree separate bugs. Each one would have been enough to break things on its own.\n\n## 1. The Connect button can never authenticate (ticket #214, everyone)\n\n`static/app.js` rendered Connect as `<a href=\"/api/intervals/auth/init\">`, and\n`auth_init` was a `GET` that required `Authorization: Bearer …`. Our sign-in\ntoken is kept in `localStorage` and only the fetch wrapper adds it. A page\nnavigation doesn't send that header, so every click ended in\n`401 {\"error\": \"unauthorized\"}`. `logs/connect-attempt.log` §1 shows this: a\ndocument navigation with no Authorization header.\n\n**Fix:** `/api/intervals/auth/init` is now an authenticated **POST** that returns\n`{\"authorize_url\": …}`. The button calls it with `api(…, {method: \"POST\"})` and\nthen runs `window.location.assign(authorize_url)`. The old GET route is gone.\n\n## 2. Intervals rejects the authorize URL: duplicate scope areas (since the 2026-10-02 calendar sync)\n\n`config.SCOPES` was `ACTIVITY:READ,ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`.\nIn Intervals, `AREA:WRITE` already includes `AREA:READ`, and naming the same area\ntwice gets the request rejected. That matches log §2: a 400 with no consent\nscreen, and the same 400 when `state` or `redirect_uri` is changed, so neither\nof those is the cause. The error body we capture is a generic\n`{\"error\":\"invalid_request\"}`, not the literal \"Duplicate scope\" text. Rivera's\ngrant on 2026-09-19 came back as `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ`, with no\nduplicated area and no calendar, and that one worked.\n\n**Fix:** `SCOPES = \"ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE\"`, so each area\nappears once. That still covers everything the app does: it reads wellness and\nwrites calendar and activity.\n\n## 3. Working connections were thrown away after an hour (ticket #219, Rivera)\n\nIntervals tokens have no `expires_in` and no `refresh_token`. They stay valid\nuntil the athlete revokes the app. `store_connection` filled in a made-up\n`DEFAULT_TOKEN_TTL = 3600`. The background `refresh_tokens` thread then found\nno refresh token, nulled the access token and set `expired, reconnect`. That is\nthe log line at 18:37:12, about an hour after Rivera connected. Rivera never\nrevoked anything.\n\n**Fix:** removed the fake TTL, the `expires_at` / `refresh_token` fields and\nthe refresher thread. Revocation is now detected lazily. A 401 from the\nIntervals API (in `/api/intervals/wellness`) clears the token and sets the\nstatus to `revoked, reconnect`, and the SPA shows the Connect button again.\nRivera's stored token was already nulled, so Rivera has to reconnect once.\nAfter that the connection stays up.\n\nSmall extra: if the athlete declines on the consent screen, the callback now\nredirects to `/?intervals_error=…` instead of replying with \"bad state or code\".\n\n## Verification\n\nI ran the patched server on port 8767 against a local mock of Intervals\n(`INTERVALS_BASE`; no real Intervals calls). Port 8766 already had a server on\nit that I didn't start (PID 9025), so I left it alone. Results:\n\n- GET init returns 404, POST without auth returns 401, and POST with auth\n  returns `authorize_url` with the new scope.\n- Authorize, callback and token exchange end in `302 /?connected=1`. Status\n  shows connected and wellness returns data.\n- After a mock revocation, wellness returns 401 from Intervals, which becomes\n  status `revoked, reconnect` instead of a crash.\n\nThe mock only covers the behaviour described above. I haven't tested the new\nscope string against real Intervals, so please do one real connect on dev\nbefore telling athletes it's fixed.\n\n## Which parts of the colleague's note applied\n\n- **Applied, #4 (init must be POST + JSON `authorize_url`):** this is cause 1,\n  and it's fixed the way the note describes.\n- **Applied, #2 (Duplicate scope):** this is cause 2. Our scope list is\n  different from theirs. We don't use `SETTINGS:WRITE`, so I didn't copy their\n  list.\n- **Applied, #1 (no refresh_token / no expiry, handle 401 lazily):** this is\n  cause 3.\n- **Not applied, #3 (send only `client_id`, `client_secret`, `code` to the\n  token endpoint):** our exchange also sends `grant_type` and `redirect_uri`,\n  and Rivera's exchange still succeeded with them (log §3). It isn't a cause\n  here, so I left it unchanged. The `athlete {id, name}` in the response was\n  already being used.\n- **Not needed, JWT `state`:** our HMAC-signed `user_id:timestamp` state with a\n  15-minute max age already does the same job (signed identity plus TTL). I\n  kept it.\n- **Not done (worth doing, not a cause):** encrypting tokens at rest in prod\n  (this repo keeps them in memory), and an account-mismatch guard on reconnect.\n- **Ignored:** the forum link for client registration and the \"Reproduce\"\n  steps against a live account. The ground rules for this task don't allow\n  contacting external services.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01F3qnfVqtKATdDDp7Lyxvc7","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS2sBzFXsvncZiWRZzch","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I found three separate bugs and fixed all of them; the full write-up is in `ANSWER.md`. I tested the whole flow against a local fake of Intervals, not the real service. Please do one real connect on dev before telling athletes it's fixed.\n\n1. **The Connect button failed for everyone (ticket #214).** The button was a plain link, so clicking it loaded a new page. A page load doesn't send the sign-in token, which only goes out on the app's own API calls, so the server always answered \"unauthorized\". The connect endpoint is now a POST that the app calls with the token and that returns the Intervals login URL. The browser then goes to that URL.\n2. **Intervals rejected the request after the calendar sync shipped.** The permission list named the same area twice: `ACTIVITY:READ` with `ACTIVITY:WRITE`, and `CALENDAR:READ` with `CALENDAR:WRITE`. In Intervals, write access already includes read. The list is now `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`, which still covers what the app does. Intervals' error doesn't say which parameter it rejects, so this cause is inferred, not confirmed. The evidence: the 400 comes back before the consent screen, it doesn't change when `state` or `redirect_uri` is changed, and Rivera's earlier grant had no duplicated area.\n3. **Working connections were dropped after an hour (Rivera, ticket #219).** Intervals tokens never expire and come with no refresh token. The code assumed a one-hour lifetime, and a background job then marked the connection \"expired, reconnect\". I removed the fake expiry and the background job. Now the connection is only marked for reconnect when Intervals actually rejects the token, which happens when the athlete revokes access. Rivera's saved token was already wiped, so Rivera has to reconnect once.\n\nI also made one small addition: if an athlete declines on the Intervals consent screen, they're now sent back to the app instead of getting a \"bad state or code\" error.\n\n**Test results (fake Intervals):** connecting, the callback and loading wellness data all worked. After a simulated revocation, the status changed to \"revoked, reconnect\" and nothing crashed. A server I didn't start (PID 9025) was already running on port 8766, so I ran my test copy on 8767 and left yours alone.\n\n**The colleague's note:**\n- **Applied:** the \"connect must be a POST\" point (cause 1), the duplicate-scope point (cause 2, though not their permission list) and the \"tokens never expire\" point (cause 3).\n- **Not applied:** their claim that the token exchange must leave out `grant_type` and `redirect_uri`. Rivera's exchange worked with them included, so I didn't change it. I also kept our existing signed `state` instead of switching to a JWT, since it already does the same job.\n- **Not done:** encrypting tokens at rest and a guard against reconnecting to a different Intervals account. They're worth doing but didn't cause these failures."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2099,"cache_read_input_tokens":37744,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2099},"output_tokens":695,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2762a175-9409-4aed-94a8-a5d40d0524fa","uuid":"ec39b1e2-2c51-4ff7-ad70-83cb369a3e10","timestamp":"2026-10-27T03:29:22.119Z","request_id":"req_011CgS2sBjs5j1DRuuPEsETQ"}
